---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	title="HelpNow+: Making Employee Support Feel Like Help • Case Study"
	breadcrumbs={["VMware", "HelpNow+", "Lead Product Designer"]}
>
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 1: HERO & TITLE                               -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<!-- Eyebrow -->
		<div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 mb-4">
			<small class="eyebrow">Tushar Roy // Case Study // HelpNow+</small>
		</div>

		<!-- Main Title -->
		<h1>Making Employee Support Feel Like Help</h1>

		<!-- Lead Paragraph -->
		<div class="max-w-4xl mb-10">
			<p>
				In a workplace known for happiness, employee support was getting
				in the way of it.
			</p>
			<p>
				At VMware, employees had to navigate a generic IT service
				management system to get help from IT, HR, Finance, Facilities,
				and other teams. I led the end-to-end design of <strong
					>HelpNow+</strong
				>, a new enterprise service experience that brought those
				services together, reduced the effort of getting help, and gave
				employees more confidence in what would happen next.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Hero Visual -->
		<figure class="m-0 mb-4">
			<div class="media-frame">
				<img
					src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20Home.png"
					alt="HelpNow+ employee home showing search, popular services, knowledge, and recent tickets"
					loading="eager"
				/>
			</div>
			<figcaption>
				The HelpNow+ employee home brings search, service discovery, and
				ticket follow-up into one unified surface.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>

		<!-- Metadata Matrix Box: Role and Timeline Panel -->
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-16">
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">role</small>
				<h4>Lead Product Designer</h4>
				<p>Service strategy, research, design system, and execution.</p>
				<small class="eyebrow"
					><time>2021 Q2</time> - <time>2023 Q1</time></small
				>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">team &amp; scale</small>
				<h4>Global Enterprise</h4>
				<p>
					Me, 2 designers, 12+ engineers, 1 PM, and 3 analysts across
					40,000+ employees globally.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<small class="kicker">outcome</small>
				<h4>94% CSAT // -40%</h4>
				<p>
					94% positive experience (n=178), ~40% quarterly escalation
					drop, 91% CSAT.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 2: CONTEXT                                    -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">context</small>
				<h2>The system worked for IT. The workplace needed more.</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					The existing service management tool had been built around
					IT processes, then stretched to cover a growing,
					geographically distributed company. For non-IT teams, the
					experience felt like an afterthought. Employees encountered
					inconsistent processes, confusing labels, and controls that
					looked alike but behaved differently.
				</p>
				<p>
					The consequences showed up in both adoption and operations.
					The case study research found that many employees avoided
					the system for non-IT needs and relied on phone or email
					instead. Agents then had to manually sort requests, while
					escalations added cost and slowed resolution.
				</p>

				<!-- Side by Side: Legacy Before & Survey Findings -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn5_before.png"
								alt="Legacy ITSM portal before HelpNow+"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Legacy ITSM portal: generic, IT-centric layouts that
							alienated non-technical departments.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/user-problem.png"
								alt="Research findings showing the employee support problem"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Survey data: 84% bypassed the portal to contact
							agents directly via phone or email.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					The experience made routine requests feel harder than
					contacting an agent directly, causing massive operational
					churn.
				</figcaption>

				<!-- Full Width: Cross-functional Ecosystem Map -->
				<div class="pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn3_ecosystem.png"
								alt="HelpNow+ enterprise service ecosystem across IT, HR, Finance, and Facilities"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Enterprise service ecosystem mapping the intake,
							triage, and fulfillment boundaries across IT, HR,
							Finance, and Facilities.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 3: THE PROBLEM                                -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">problem</small>
				<h2>Too many choices created a false path</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					I began with a heuristic review and interviews with
					employees and support teams. People spent more than two
					minutes trying to find the right service. When the catalog
					was hard to understand, experienced users took a shortcut:
					they submitted a generic incident instead of finding the
					right request. That looked faster in the moment, but it
					created extra triage work downstream.
				</p>
				<p>
					The product had to serve occasional users who needed clear
					guidance and frequent users who valued speed. It also had to
					work across IT, HR, Finance, Real Estate, and Workplace
					teams, each with different policies and service workflows.
				</p>

				<!-- User Cohort Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<h4>Occasional Users</h4>
						<p>
							Needed clear visual hierarchy, guided navigation,
							and recognizable service categories to locate
							unfamiliar workflows without getting overwhelmed.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<h4>Frequent Users &amp; Triage</h4>
						<p>
							Valued speed above all, but resorting to generic
							incident forms created an expensive operational
							bottleneck for support triage teams.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Heuristic Analysis Visual -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-2">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-helpnow/hn2_heuristic.png"
							alt="Heuristic review of the original HelpNow experience"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						Comprehensive heuristic review: surfacing navigation
						overload, weak action hierarchy, ambiguous terminology,
						and employee privacy concerns.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Alignment & Ideation Process Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/alignment.png"
								alt="Stakeholder alignment and mapping sessions"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Cross-functional alignment: aligning IT, HR, and
							operations leadership on service level expectations.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/sketching.png"
								alt="Exploratory sketching and early paper wireframes"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Exploratory sketching: rapid paper prototyping
							testing different discovery and search intake
							models.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Bridging business alignment and tactile design explorations
					to redefine the core information architecture.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 4: HYPOTHESIS & FIRST ATTEMPT                 -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">hypothesis</small>
				<h2>We tried search without the map</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Our first hypothesis was simple: if people came to the
					portal looking for help, let them describe what they needed.
					We explored a search-first home backed by NLP and Elastic
					Search, stripping the interface back to a single input. The
					engineering proof of concept showed that intent detection
					could route people toward relevant services.
				</p>
				<p>
					But the early design asked users to recall what the system
					could do without showing them the available services. In
					testing, people felt lost. They asked where the catalog had
					gone and whether the system was broken. Search made the page
					look simpler, but it removed the cues people needed to
					understand their options.
				</p>

				<!-- Trade-off Card: Jakob's Law -->
				<div class="card my-8">
					<small class="eyebrow mb-2"
						>Jakob’s Law: Recognition Over Recall</small
					>
					<h4>A Single Search Field Removed Necessary Cues</h4>
					<p>
						While a minimal search box looked clean, it forced users
						to guess what services existed. Discovery broke down
						because employees couldn't formulate search terms for
						services they didn't know existed.
					</p>
				</div>

				<!-- Images: First Cut and Feedback Side by Side -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn4_first_cut.png"
								alt="First search-first concept"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							The first concept relied on a minimalist search
							input as the primary entry point.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/hn4_first_failure.png"
								alt="Early test feedback on the search-first design"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Usability feedback: users felt completely lost
							without visible category anchors.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>

				<!-- Usability Failure Breakdown Visual -->
				<div class="pt-6">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/failure.png"
								alt="Usability test observations and design failure analysis"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Test observation breakdown: documenting why 72% of
							participants stalled when presented with an empty
							search state.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Validating early and failing fast prevented costly
					engineering on a pure search approach that ignored human
					cognitive patterns.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 5: THE PIVOT                                  -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">strategy</small>
				<h2>
					The pivot: discovery when you need it, speed when you know
				</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					We brought the service catalog back and kept search as a
					fast path. The resulting hybrid model gave employees visible
					categories and popular services, while shortcuts such as
					recent searches, frequently used services, and pins helped
					repeat users move quickly.
				</p>
				<p>
					That balance made the home more useful to both kinds of
					users: browse when the right service is unfamiliar; jump
					directly to it when it is known. The final experience also
					brought knowledge and ticket status closer to the point where
					employees were deciding what to do next.
				</p>

				<!-- Hybrid Model Highlight Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<h4>Discovery: Visible Catalog</h4>
						<p>
							Curated categories, popular service cards, and
							suggested knowledge articles for employees exploring
							new or unfamiliar departmental needs.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<h4>Speed: Smart Shortcuts</h4>
						<p>
							NLP-powered search, recent requests, pinned
							favorites, and direct one-click re-orders for
							experienced and frequent users.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Image: Final HelpNow+ Employee Home -->
				<figure class="m-0 pt-2">
					<div class="media-frame">
						<img
							src="/images-helpnow/hn4_final_employee.png"
							alt="Final HelpNow+ employee home"
						/>
					</div>
					<figcaption>
						The final direction pairs visible service discovery with
						intelligent search and personalized shortcuts.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>

				<!-- Employee Experience Deep Dive: Asymmetric 12-Column Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-12 gap-5 pt-6">
					<div class="sm:col-span-7">
						<figure
							class="m-0 h-full flex flex-col justify-between"
						>
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20Service%20Catalog.png"
									alt="HelpNow+ full browseable service catalog"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								Full Service Catalog: browsable taxonomies
								across IT, HR, Finance, and Facilities with
								instant filters.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
					</div>

					<div class="sm:col-span-5 flex flex-col gap-5">
						<figure class="m-0 flex-1">
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20Home%20%28Catalogs%20Dropdown%20Open%29.png"
									alt="Quick catalog navigation dropdown on home"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								Catalog Dropdown: quick drawer previewing
								services without leaving the homepage.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>

						<figure class="m-0 flex-1">
							<div class="media-frame">
								<img
									src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20My%20Tickets.png"
									alt="Employee ticket tracking dashboard"
								/>
							</div>
							<figcaption>
								My Tickets: clear visibility into open and
								historical requests with real-time status.
							</figcaption>
						</figure>
					</div>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Combining fast search indexing with structured visual
					catalog browsing and contextual request tracking.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 6: DESIGN - TRUST & PRIVACY                   -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">design</small>
				<h2>Trust had to be visible before submit</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Navigation was only part of the adoption problem. Employees
					told us they avoided the portal for sensitive HR requests
					because they could not tell who would see their ticket.
					Without clear privacy controls, calling or emailing an agent
					felt safer.
				</p>
				<p>
					We made privacy and accountability part of the interface. A <strong
						>Private Mode</strong
					> control let employees choose restricted visibility before submitting
					a sensitive request. We also added a direct <strong
						>Escalate</strong
					> action to ticket details, giving people a clear next step when
					a request stalled instead of making them resort to side channels
					preemptively.
				</p>
				<p>
					The platform limited how much of the existing interface we
					could customize. For the ticket details experience, we
					designed a custom portal on top of the underlying system so
					we could make these controls clear without requiring a broad
					backend replacement.
				</p>

				<!-- Key Feature Cards -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">CONFIDENTIALITY</small>
						<h4>Private Mode</h4>
						<p>
							Explicit visibility toggles allow employees to
							submit confidential HR, compensation, or personal
							inquiries with verifiable discretion.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">REASSURANCE</small>
						<h4>Direct Escalation Action</h4>
						<p>
							Transparent status progression and a built-in
							escalate button prevent premature calls and urgent
							side-channel emails.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Side by Side: Ticket Detail & Admin Permissions -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5 pt-2">
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Employee%20-%20Ticket%20Detail%20%28Simple%29.png"
								alt="Employee ticket detail experience with private mode and escalation"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Employee Ticket Detail: surfaces Private Mode
							status, updates timeline, SLA countdown, and
							one-click Escalate action.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Admin%20-%20Permissions.png"
								alt="Admin permissions and role-based privacy access controls"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							Underlying Governance: granular role-based access
							enforcing privacy boundaries between departments.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					Trust made tangible: requesters control who views their
					ticket, backed by verifiable system-level permissions.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 7: FULFILLER WORKSPACE                        -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">execution</small>
				<h2>Empowering fulfillers with a unified workspace</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					Support agents previously juggled disparate ticketing
					dashboards with poor search, ambiguous SLAs, and no
					contextual history. We designed a focused fulfiller
					workspace tailored to high-velocity task execution.
				</p>
				<p>
					The interface organizes tasks around ownership, urgency, and
					service context. Agents can see immediately what requires
					action, assess team workloads, track SLA burn-down rates, and
					resolve complex requests without leaving their primary view.
				</p>

				<!-- Six Fulfiller Workflows: 2x3 / 3x2 Grid -->
				<div
					class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4 pt-4"
				>
					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20Dashboard.png"
								alt="Fulfiller operational overview dashboard"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							01. Overview — High-level queue analytics, active
							caseloads, and daily resolution targets.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fullfiller%20-%20My%20Tasks.png"
								alt="Personal task queue prioritized by urgency and SLA"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							02. My Tasks — Personal task queue sorted by SLA
							urgency and escalation status.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20My%20Group%20Tasks.png"
								alt="Team triage queue for unassigned incoming tickets"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							03. Group Tasks — Departmental triage queue for team
							leads to balance workload.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20Ticket%20Detail%20%28Onboarding%29.png"
								alt="Detailed agent resolution view for employee onboarding"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							04. Execution — Multi-stage fulfillment view with
							integrated approval checkpoints.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20Service%20Health.png"
								alt="Real-time service health and queue backlog monitoring"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							05. Service Health — Live queue velocity metrics to
							detect operational bottlenecks early.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>

					<figure class="m-0">
						<div class="media-frame">
							<img
								src="/images-helpnow/Fulfiller%20-%20Reports%20List.png"
								alt="Operational reporting and SLA compliance list"
							/>
						</div>
						<figcaption>
							06. Reporting — Historical SLA compliance, CSAT
							feedback, and resolution times.
						</figcaption>
					</figure>
				</div>
				<figcaption class="mt-2">
					A comprehensive fulfillment suite: from personal task
					execution to real-time service health and compliance
					analytics.
				</figcaption>
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>

	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<!-- SECTION 8: SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE & ADMIN BUILDER        -->
	<!-- ===================================================== -->
	<article class="case-section">
		<div class="case-grid">
			<!-- Left Column -->
			<div class="case-sidebar">
				<small class="kicker">architecture</small>
				<h2>One service, three connected experiences</h2>
			</div>

			<!-- Right Column -->
			<div class="case-content">
				<p>
					A request is not a single form. Employees need to know what
					they asked for and what is happening. Fulfillers need to see
					the work, its priority, and the next action. Service owners
					need to define the intake, routing, approvals, and completion
					criteria.
				</p>
				<p>
					We designed across that full lifecycle. For administrators,
					guided service creation connected service details, intake
					forms, workflow logic, and review before publication. This
					helped make the service itself easier to maintain, not just
					easier to find.
				</p>

				<!-- 3 Lifecycle Roles Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-8">
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">01. REQUESTERS</small>
						<h4>Employee Intake</h4>
						<p>
							Unified catalog, intuitive forms, transparent status
							updates, and explicit privacy controls.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">02. FULFILLERS</small>
						<h4>Task Workspace</h4>
						<p>
							Queue prioritized by urgency and SLA, clear
							ownership, and inline customer context.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card">
						<small class="eyebrow">03. SERVICE OWNERS</small>
						<h4>Workflow Builder</h4>
						<p>
							Guided 4-step creation wizard connecting intake,
							routing rules, approvals, and sign-off.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<!-- Step-by-Step Guided Service Creation: 4-Step Grid -->
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-2">
